  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nI will implement an optimization algorithm combining local search and population-based methods to minimize the objective function. The approach will discard the previous progressive doubling strategy and instead focus on maintaining a population of candidate sequences at a fixed length (512) while allowing occasional mutations to explore the solution space. This method differs from the previous approach by:\n- Eliminating stages and phase transitions\n- Maintaining a single population of sequences\n- Using probabilistic selection of neighborhoods\n- Employing adaptive random restarts as the primary optimization mechanism\n- Allowing controlled mutations to maintain diversity\n\nThis differs significantly from the strategies used before, which involved:\n- Progressive is Length-based optimization (256\u2192512\u21921024)\n- Strict interpolation between different length sequences\n- Complex fallback systems based on sum normalization\n- Separate phases for LP optimization and Adam polishing\n\nThe new approach treats the optimization as a single cohesive process with randomized exploration, making it fundamentally different. It prioritizes broad exploration over structured progression and uses probabilistic mutation selection as a core principle.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nThe optimization will occur in a space of sequences of fixed length L=512. Each sequence is represented as a vector of non-negative floats a[0..L-1].\n\nThe objective function is defined as using `evaluate_sequence` which computes:\nscore = 2 * L * (max(b_sequence) + 1e-12) / (float(sum(a))+1e-12)**2\nWhere b_sequence = linear convolution(a, a) - min linear convolution(a, a) (to make max position more stable)\n\nAll sequences will be normalized to have sum >0.01 before evaluation.\n\nOperations will be performed via the evaluate_sequence function for simplicity and consistency, though this could introduce evaluation overhead.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nI will implement a fixed-length population-based search algorithm with the following steps:\n\nInitialize:\n  - Set fixed length L=512\n  - Define population size N (around 100)\n  - Initialize population with N random sequences (each entry between 0 and 1000) but normalized so sum >=0.01\n  - Set mutation probabilities for basic operations:\n      - 85% chance: 10% position change\n      - 10% chance: 10% amplitude change\n      - 5% chance: 10% duration change\n\nAlgorithm loop (up to 1775 seconds):\n  a. Evaluate population using `evaluate_sequence`\n  b. Apply selection:\n      - Identify best performer (lowest score)\n      - Select top 20% (19 out of 50) for various probabilities\n\n  c. Apply randomization:\n      - Always maintain at least one copy of the current best survivor (to avoid optimization loss)\n      - Elsewhere apply probabilistic mutation:\n          - 85% of mutations: position change in small blocks (each of size 64, change amplitude in 10% of blocks)\n          - 10% of mutations: amplitude change (small random noise)\n          - 5% of mutations: duration change (expanding time window for entire sequence)\n\n  d. New sequence creation always maintains a1/b\u2081ance 1000 bound, non-negativity and sum 0.01 bound.\n\n  e. Every N_indv mutations (about 50 per LBFGS call) uses the clone of the best performer for a local robust optimization using the LBFGS algorithm (from scipy.optimize) but this is complex.\n\nAssociate lifecycle of each candidate:\n\n  i. After initial population:\n      - Select one best survivor per generation\n\n  ii. After each generation:\n      - Take one clone of the best and mutate it using the chosen neighborhood operations.\n      - For each new candidate, we need to check for validity and store it\n      - Maintain a history of the best ever encountered\n\nTermination condition:\n  - After exceeding 1775 seconds (or aim for 30 minutes remaining for final evaluation)\n  - We then perform a thorough evaluation of the best survivor\n  - Final score is computed independently using the author's evaluator\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nWe will sample from three local optimization methods (LBFGS, Stochastic Search, GP-based) but only allow one active at any time.\n\nStandard parameters:\n  - L=512\n  - N=100\n  - Random seed=42\n  - Mutation probabilities:\n      pos_change: 0.85\n      amp_change: 0.10 (varied by 10%)\n      dur_change: 0.05 (expanded time window)\n\nPrimary search strategy:\n  We'll use a simple random local search until the best candidate improves by a significant margin (0.01 level) or time expires.\n\nSchedule:\n  - Primary loop: 95% position change, 5% extremal move\n  - Stamina: 800 seconds\n  - Enhancements: genetic drift at 15% for very large changes\n\nBut this needs to be actionable.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nWe have about 1775 seconds.\n\nWe will:\n  - Allocate approximately maximize_one_optimize = 1300 seconds for optimization\n  - 475 seconds for final neighborhood polish\n  - 50 seconds for fallback worst-case\n\nFallback conditions:\n  - If local optimization fails to find improvement for 10 consecutive generations\n  - We will reset with a small perturbation from the best ever\n\nComplexity note:\n  Each candidate evaluation requires L=512 O(N^2) time using numpy convolve intercept\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nWe must validate the fallback candidate: if-normalization produced only zeros, use gradient descent from the last candidate.\n\nFinal output procedure:\n  - Return the best candidate sequence maintained by the search\n  - Convert to Python list of floats\n  - Clip values to [0,1000]\n  - Include metadata about the found score\n\nWe must compute the score independently for the final sequence.\n\nAfter this analysis, we'll proceed with the implementation.\n\nFor the coder agent, I leave only:\n\n  def propose_candidate(budget_s=1800):\n      n = 512\n      initial_sequence = np.maximum(make_linear_profile(n